+/* ---- the screenshot: the picture as the window shows it --------------------
+ * F12 in debug mode and the dump trigger file (PS3RECOMP_METAL_DUMP_TRIGGER)
+ * dump the guest's render targets, which are not what the window shows: no
+ * FXAA/SMAA, no supersampling reduction or MetalFX, no sharpening, gamma,
+ * colour effects or menu. Both also take a screenshot of the next frame that
+ * goes to the screen, after the present pass and the overlay, at the
+ * drawable's size: <PS3RECOMP_METAL_SURF_DUMP>/screen_<date>-<time>.png. The
+ * copy is a blit on the frame's own command buffer into a shared buffer; the
+ * PNG is written from its completion, on a queue of its own (never on the
+ * render thread).
+ *
+ * A drawable can be read only when the layer's framebufferOnly is NO, which
+ * gives up the display-only layout the system may otherwise pick for it. So
+ * the layer stays framebufferOnly = YES as it always was, and a pending shot
+ * turns it NO (on the main thread) until the PNG is written: the frames in
+ * between, usually one or two, are skipped by the shot. PS3_FRAMEGEN's real
+ * picture (a private texture the pacer copies) and the headless target are
+ * readable as they are. With F12 the menu's "captured" notice comes once the
+ * file is written, so it is not in the picture. */
+static volatile int s_shot_want;      /* the next frame shown is captured */
+static volatile int s_shot_notice;    /* ... asked by F12: say so when written */
+static volatile int s_shot_readable;  /* we turned framebufferOnly off for it */
+static u32 s_shot_wait;               /* presents that found no readable drawable */
